{"seconds": 56.9, "exit": 0, "final": "After Ne8+, if Black plays Rxe8 or Qxe8, the knight is captured. But wait\u2014what about Kg8? Then the knight is still attacked. This line is getting complicated. Let me simplify and verify the main winning line one more time:\n\n**1. Nf6+** is the key move because:\n- If Black plays **Kh8**, then **2. Qh3+** forces the king, and if **2...Qh7**, then **3. Qxh7#** is checkmate.\n- If Black plays **Kg7**, then White can play **2. Nxe4** winning the knight, or pursue checks.\n- If Black captures the knight with **Qxf6** or **Nxf6**, White wins material back.\n\nThe clearest line to show is: **1. Nf6+ Kh8 2. Qh3+ Qh7 3. Qxh7#**\n\nThis is a **fork** (and attack on two pieces) combined with a mating pattern.\n\nNow let me write the coaching note and format the answer:\n\n```json\n{\"move\": \"Nf6+\", \"line\": [\"Nf6+\", \"Kh8\", \"Qh3+\", \"Qh7\", \"Qxh7#\"], \"motifs\": [\"fork\", \"backRankMate\"]}\n```\n\n**Coaching Note:**\n\nYour knight check on f6 forks the king and e4 knight\u2014Black's most active piece. After 1.Nf6+ Kh8 (or other king moves lose the knight), 2.Qh3+ forces the queen to h7 to block, and 3.Qxh7# is checkmate because the king is trapped by its own pawns. Look for checks that attack undefended pieces: they often lead to forced wins.", "tool_calls": [{"tool": "skill", "args": {"skill": "chess-coach"}}, {"tool": "chess-eyes-board", "args": {"fen": "5rk1/2p2q2/1p1p2p1/1P1P4/1P2n1N1/r7/6QP/6RK w - - 0 37"}}, {"tool": "chess-eyes-threats", "args": {"fen": "5rk1/2p2q2/1p1p2p1/1P1P4/1P2n1N1/r7/6QP/6RK w - - 0 37"}}, {"tool": "chess-eyes-legal_moves", "args": {"fen": "5rk1/2p2q2/1p1p2p1/1P1P4/1P2n1N1/r7/6QP/6RK w - - 0 37"}}, {"tool": "chess-eyes-inspect_move", "args": {"fen": "5rk1/2p2q2/1p1p2p1/1P1P4/1P2n1N1/r7/6QP/6RK w - - 0 37", "move": "Nh6+"}}, {"tool": "chess-eyes-inspect_move", "args": {"fen": "5rk1/2p2q2/1p1p2p1/1P1P4/1P2n1N1/r7/6QP/6RK w - - 0 37", "move": "Nf6+"}}, {"tool": "chess-eyes-inspect_move", "arg